        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WATCHPM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Penman-Monteith equation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Potential evaporation is available from the CRU and the WATCH datasets. Both the CRU and the WATCH datasets provide estimates based on the Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) reference-crop-evaporation equation (Allen et al., 1994). In this version of the Penman\u2013Monteith equation (Monteith, 1965), a hypothetical well-watered reference crop is defined with a height of 0.12m, an albedo of 0.23, a surface resistance of 70sm\u00af\u00b9 and an aerodynamic resistance based on the crop height and wind speed. The WATCH FAO Penman-Monteith (WATCHPM) dataset is based on 3-hourly bias-corrected ERA-40 data (Weedon et al., 2011).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CRU TS 3.10.01",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Penman-Monteith equation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Potential evaporation is available from the CRU and the WATCH datasets. Both the CRU and the WATCH datasets provide estimates based on the Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) reference-crop-evaporation equation (Allen et al., 1994). In this version of the Penman\u2013Monteith equation (Monteith, 1965), a hypothetical well-watered reference crop is defined with a height of 0.12m, an albedo of 0.23, a surface resistance of 70sm\u00af\u00b9 and an aerodynamic resistance based on the crop height and wind speed. The CRU estimates were calculated from monthly gridded values of average/minimum/maximum temperatures, vapour pressure and cloud cover, and fixed monthly wind speeds for the standard period 1961-1990 (Harris et al., 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WATCHPT",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Priestley-Taylor equation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The WATCH dataset also provides estimates based on the Priestley-Taylor equation (Priestley and Taylor, 1972) assuming an albedo of 0.23 and the a factor set to 1.26 (Weedon et al., 2011).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WATCH precipitation data",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "INHERITED_FROM",
            "target_entity_instance": "ERA-40",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "As opposed to the CRU and GPCC precipitation datasets, the WATCH precipitation data are not based on observed data, but on the European Centre for Medium-Range Forecasts (ECMWF) 40 yr reanalysis, ERA-40 (Uppala et al., 2005). However, two earlier versions of the CRU (version 2.1) and GPCC (version 4) data products have been used to correct ERA-40 precipitation for known biases (e.g. Hagemann et al., 2005).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
